      "problem": "${(1+6t)}^{7}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=6t$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+6t)}^{7} & =1+7\\cdot 6t+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(6t)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+42t+\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2}\\cdot 36{t}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+42t+21\\cdot 36{t}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+42t+756{t}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
