      "worked_solution": "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\frac{2k}{3}$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{2k}{3})}^{7} & =1+7\\cdot (-\\frac{2k}{3})+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{2k}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4{k}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+21\\cdot \\frac{4{k}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{14k}{3}+\\frac{28{k}^{2}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
